                "text": "Find a face diagonal, then apply Pythagoras to that diagonal and a perpendicular edge.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $f$ be a face diagonal and $d$ the longest diagonal, both measured in metres. By Pythagoras on a square face:\n$$\\begin{align*}f^2&=x^2+x^2\\\\f^2&=2x^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe face diagonal and a perpendicular edge form a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is the longest diagonal.\n$$\\begin{align*}d^2&=f^2+x^2\\\\d^2&=2x^2+x^2\\\\d^2&=3x^2\\\\d&=x\\sqrt{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe longest diagonal is $x\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
